"delivery.despatchAddress","What delivery address does the merchant's own despatch, shipment or order record show the merchandise was sent to? Read the postal address only — street, town, postcode, country. Do not take it from a carrier's record, from correspondence, or from the merchant's account of the case. NOT_FOUND where no such record shows one.","value","DOCUMENT","orderRecord","MERCHANT","FREE_TEXT","c31fb956dd5",53,NULL
"delivery.agreedDeliveryDate","What delivery or service date did the order promise?","value","DOCUMENT","orderRecord","EITHER","ISO_DATE","c14b3ae65ac",49,NULL
"delivery.status","What does the delivery record show happened to this shipment — DELIVERED, RETURNED_TO_SENDER or IN_TRANSIT? Read the status off the delivery record itself: the carrier's or the merchant's own system record of the delivery event, written as a by-product of that event. **An account of what a record says is not the record**, whoever wrote it and however confident it is — illustrative, not a closed list: correspondence with the cardholder, a complaint, a case comment, a rebuttal or covering letter, a summary prepared for this dispute. Answer NOT_FOUND where only such an account exists, and where the record itself does not show a status. What is excluded is a party's own narration of a status — a sentence saying the goods arrived, a summary, either side's account of what some record shows — and not a record reproduced inside such a letter: a tracking table, a status field of a carrier's or a fulfilment system's record, a screenshot of one, or a delivery log pasted in as an exhibit is still that record, so read it and quote the exhibit's own span. Judge each span by what produced it, never by the document it is embedded in.","value","DOCUMENT","proofOfDelivery","MERCHANT","ENUM(DELIVERED,RETURNED_TO_SENDER,IN_TRANSIT)","c10578fd7f1",55,NULL
"merchant.certifiesCardholderTookMerchandiseAtSale","Does the merchant's certification state that the Cardholder took the merchandise at the time of sale? The document must assert that the goods were handed over. A record that evidences the sale rather than the handover is not a certification — illustrative, not a closed list: a signed sales receipt, an invoice, a till record. Read only what the certification states; do not infer handover from the transaction being card-present or from the sale having completed. Answer NOT_FOUND where no certification is present.","truth","DOCUMENT","merchantCertification","MERCHANT",NULL,"c4c3a60b095",56,NULL
"service.attendedAtAgreedLocation","Does this document contain a record, created as a by-product of the attendance itself rather than of the booking, showing the Cardholder or an authorized person present at the place the service was to be rendered? The document may hold several records and the merchant's own account; look for the record of presence and ignore the transaction's own charge or authorisation line. Illustrative, not a closed list — a card-present tender at the merchant's premises with a time, table or terminal, a check-in or gate scan, a signed attendance or collection record. A booking, a reservation or a seat allocation is not attendance, and a merchant's own statement that the Cardholder attended is not a record of it. This asks about a service. A record of a sale of goods is not a record of a service attended, whatever else the same receipt shows: a tender, card-verification or authentication line on such a receipt evidences how the payment was proved genuine, not that anyone attended anywhere for a service. A card-present tender stays a record of presence where the record it sits in is a record of a service rendered at that place. Where one record carries both a service line and a tender line, answer from the service line and cite that. Answer NOT_FOUND where the only record is a sale of goods, or a payment authentication and nothing more. Answer TRUE where a record of presence is present; FALSE where the records show only a booking, or show the Cardholder did not attend; NOT_FOUND where no such record is present. Cite the line of the record that shows the presence, not a sentence describing it.","truth","DOCUMENT","serviceRecord","MERCHANT",NULL,"cce244679f5",57,NULL
"service.futureServicesAvailable","Does this document contain the merchant's own booking, reservation or scheduling record for this transaction showing the service held available for the cardholder — active, not cancelled by the merchant, running as scheduled — and not consumed? The document may hold several records and the merchant's own account; look for the booking or scheduling record. This asks whether the service was available to be used, not whether it was used: a record showing the service used, attended or flown is a record of use, belongs to a different question, and answers FALSE here. Illustrative, not closed lists — available: a booking or reservation confirmed and held, a ticket or voucher valid and open for use, a seat allocated and unused, a reservation recorded as a no-show. Not available: cancelled or withdrawn by the merchant, refused, the flight or event not operated. The records may be read together: a booking or reservation record for what was held, and a system status or scheduling record for whether it was cancelled or operated. Answer TRUE where they show the booking active and not cancelled by the merchant through the agreed service date and no record in the document shows the service consumed; FALSE where a record shows it consumed, or cancelled or withdrawn by the merchant; NOT_FOUND where no booking, reservation or scheduling record is present. A general statement that the service was operating, a merchant assertion that it remained available, and any reproduction of the issuer's dispute form or the cardholder's own statements inside the merchant's pack are not evidence. Cite the line of the record that shows the status, not a sentence describing it.","truth","DOCUMENT","serviceRecord","MERCHANT",NULL,"c790643f7f4",59,NULL
"service.usageEvidencedOnRecord","Does this document contain a record, created by the system that performed the service as a by-product of the service being used, showing that the service was used? The document may hold several records and the merchant's own account; look for the record of the use event and ignore the transaction's own charge or authorisation line, which is neither use nor provision. This asks about a service. A record of merchandise being delivered, collected or handed over is a record of delivery, not of a service being used; answer NOT_FOUND on it. A record of use cannot exist unless the use occurred; a record of provision exists the moment the merchant issues the thing. Illustrative, not closed lists — use: a check-in, a boarding scan, a coupon or ticket status showing it was flown or redeemed, baggage acceptance, a trip or ride record, a till tender for an in-person service, a folio charge, a session or login record. Provision: a booking confirmed, a ticket or voucher issued, a confirmation emailed, a seat allocated, a reservation held. Answer TRUE where a record of use is present; FALSE where the records show only provision, or show the service unused (a no-show, unused, open for use); NOT_FOUND where no service record is present. Cite the line of the record that shows the use event, not a sentence describing it.","truth","DOCUMENT","serviceRecord","MERCHANT",NULL,"c4e0fbc9c78",60,NULL
"service.cancellationDate","What date does the document show the Merchant or service provider canceled the goods or services?","value","DOCUMENT","correspondence","EITHER","ISO_DATE","c4eed905eb5",58,NULL
"cardholder.confirmedResolvedInMerchantRecords","Do the merchant's records contain written communication with the Cardholder in which the Cardholder states that the matter is resolved, that they have been refunded and are content, or that they are withdrawing the dispute? Count only written exchanges where the Cardholder's own words appear — not merchant assertions about the Cardholder, not call notes or internal records, and not inferred satisfaction. Answer TRUE where such a statement in the Cardholder's own words is present, and cite the span that carries it. Answer FALSE where the merchant's records are present and carry no such statement: a record that is there and silent on resolution is an answer, not an absence — and that includes a record evidencing a refund, a credit, a reversal or a settlement, which is the merchant's system speaking and not the Cardholder. Answer UNRESOLVED only where the material is unreadable, or where it contradicts itself about whether the Cardholder said this.","truth","MODEL","correspondence","MERCHANT",NULL,"c5bf9598672",61,NULL
"issuer.describesComplaintAsFlightNotOperated","Does the Issuer's dispute material record the Cardholder complaining that the FLIGHT DID NOT TAKE PLACE, as opposed to that they were denied boarding, missed it, or had their booking cancelled? Judge only from issuer-supplied dispute material — never from the merchant's flight record. Answer FALSE where the material contains no such complaint — a description that names only the flight or the purchase has not complained that it did not take place; give the reason rather than a quote. Answer UNRESOLVED only where the material is unreadable or contradicts itself.","truth","MODEL","issuerDisputeQuestionnaire","ISSUER",NULL,"c9eaba0fd4d",63,NULL
"issuer.describesPurchaseBeyondClearingData","Does the issuer's description of what was purchased add specific information beyond what the Clearing Record already carries — the merchant name, MCC, amount, currency and date? TRUE where it identifies the actual merchandise or service. FALSE where no description is supplied at all, or where it only restates the merchant, the amount, or the fact of a dispute. **A reason-code category such as Merchandise or Services is not a description; quoting it can only support FALSE.** Judge only from issuer-supplied material.","truth","MODEL","issuerDisputeQuestionnaire","ISSUER",NULL,"c3ed4674de1",64,NULL
"issuer.describesQualityOfProvidedGoodsOrServices","Does the cardholder's complaint concern the quality of merchandise or a service that was PROVIDED to them, rather than a failure to receive it? Judge only from the Issuer's dispute description and cardholder statement. If any part of the purchase is alleged never to have arrived at all, the answer is false. The form's structured reason-code fields are the filing, not the cardholder's statement, and do not count as an allegation of non-arrival. Do not quote a structured certification field — the reason-code line, the dispute category, or the answer to ""What was not received?"" — as your support; quote the cardholder's own words where there are any. Answer FALSE where the cardholder's statement says nothing about the quality of what was provided — a bare product name or a description with no complaint sentence has not described a quality complaint; give the reason rather than a quote. Answer UNRESOLVED only where the statement is unreadable or contradicts itself.","truth","MODEL","issuerDisputeQuestionnaire","ISSUER",NULL,"c546e2fbf1e",65,NULL
"issuer.describesTransactionAsFraudulent","Does anything the Issuer submitted with this Dispute record the Cardholder stating the Transaction is fraudulent — that they did not make, authorise, or participate in it? Judge only from issuer-supplied material (dispute narrative, cardholder letter, issuer questionnaire or certification) — never from merchant records, the transaction record, or any classification of ours — and never infer fraud from the transaction, the merchant, or the amount. Answer FALSE where the issuer-supplied material contains no such statement — a description that names only what was bought, or says nothing about the Cardholder's part in the transaction, has not described it as fraudulent; give the reason rather than a quote. Answer UNRESOLVED only where the material is unreadable or contradicts itself.","truth","MODEL","issuerDisputeQuestionnaire","ISSUER",NULL,"c5b0545dc79",66,NULL
"delivery.podAddressMatchesAgreed","A is the delivery address on the carrier's proof of delivery. If A is absent, use A2 — the address on the merchant's own despatch or order record — in A's place, and begin your reason by stating that the carrier's record showed no address and the merchant's record was used. Do A and B identify the same delivery point? Compare only the components both carry, in this order: postcode or ZIP; street number; street name; town. A postcode or ZIP present on both sides must agree — if not, answer FALSE. A component present on one side only is ignored: a carrier's record may show only a town and postcode. Where the postcode or ZIP agrees, a difference in town name alone is not decisive — a carrier's record often shows the postal town rather than the locality; note the difference in the reason. Tolerate abbreviation, ordering, punctuation, case, and minor spelling variation in a street or town name where the number and postcode agree. Never infer or complete a missing component. Answer TRUE only when every component present on both sides agrees; FALSE when any does not; UNRESOLVED when the two share no component to compare, saying which side lacks what. In your reason, name each component compared and its result.","truth","FACTS",NULL,NULL,NULL,"c4503d4e4b8",68,NULL
"delivery.podContainsFullAddress","Does A carry a full delivery address by the conventions of the country the address itself indicates — a street-level line (number and street, or that country's equivalent), a locality, and a postal code where that country uses one? Determine the country from the address (postcode format, state, country line), never from the merchant. Answer TRUE or FALSE; UNRESOLVED if A is absent or unreadable. Name what is present and what is missing.","truth","FACTS",NULL,NULL,NULL,"c10660a974d",69,NULL
